  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 105 PM MDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nThe 596 to 597 dam 500 mb upper high fully pivots back west to over \nthe NM/AZ border Wednesday due a trough axis moving across the north \ncentral U.S. south of an upper low over Hudson Bay in Canada. This \ntrough looks to send a weak backdoor front through far northeast NM \nand the east slopes of the northern Sangre de Cristo Mountains come \nmidday Wednesday. Higher moisture (PWATS increasing from 1-1.2 \ninches to 1.2 to 1.4 inches) and upslope flow behind this front will \nresult in a better coverage of showers and storms across this part \nof the state early Wednesday afternoon with weak northwest flow \ndirecting storms into the northeast and east central highlands and \nplains come Wednesday evening. CAMs are also showing a better \ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms across the mountain ranges and \nnearby lower elevations along and south of I-40 Wednesday afternoon \nand evening due to higher PWATS of 1 to 1.2 inches across this area. \nNorthwest flow around the upper high looks to bring drier air into \nfar northwest and north central NM (PWATS down to 0.5 to 0.8 inches) \nmostly shutting down shower and thunderstorm activity across this \npart of the state. The upper high continues shifting west to \nsouthern CA and southwest AZ weakening to 593 to 595 dam at 500 mb \nThursday. The drier air across northwest and north central NM \nWednesday makes more progress south and east Thursday helping to \nlimit shower and storm coverage to the southern high terrain and \nnearby lower elevations along with the Caprock due to PWATS around \n0.9 to 1.3 inches and the remnants of Wednesday's backdoor front \nacross far east central NM and West Texas.\n\nThe upper high further weakens to 591 to 592 at 500 mb as it moves \nback east over southern AZ and NM. Relatively drier air looks to \nremain in place with PWATS around 0.7 to 0.9 inches thus limiting \nisolated to at most scattered storm coverage to the far western and \nsouthern high terrain. Still low confidence this weekend into early \nnext week due to differences in the placement and strength of the \nupper high as a upper low enters the Pacific NW. The deterministic \nGFS, ECMWF, and AI-ECMWF shift the upper high east of the state late \nSaturday and over the south central U.S. Sunday due to the upper low \npushing east to over the northern Rockies (Scenario #1). Meanwhile, \nthe AI-GFS and Canadian keep the upper high overhead and strengthen \nit to 594 dam Saturday and 596 to 597 dam Sunday with the ridge axis \nextending north to the northern Rockies and High Plains ad the upper \nlow remaining just off the coast of the Pacific NW (Scenario #2). \nLooking at the ensemble clusters, there is a slight lean towards \nScenario #1 over #2 (65% to 35%). Scenario #1 will result in \nslightly cooler temperatures with a monsoon plume and higher storm \ncoverage over western and central NM while Scenario #2 will result \nin hotter temperatures and lower storm coverage with higher terrain \nareas being favored.",
